<Meta
  title="Guides/03 Usage"
  summary="Set up the provider and render your first component."
/>

# Usage

Breeze UI components must be rendered inside `BreezeProvider`. Add it once, at the root of your application:

```tsx
import { BreezeProvider } from '@motech-development/breeze-ui';
import { createRoot } from 'react-dom/client';
import { App } from './App';
import '@motech-development/breeze-ui/styles.css';

const root = document.getElementById('root');

if (root) {
  createRoot(root).render(
    <BreezeProvider locale="en-GB">
      <App />
    </BreezeProvider>,
  );
}
```

Components can then be used anywhere below the provider:

```tsx
import { Button } from '@motech-development/breeze-ui';

export function SaveAction() {
  return <Button onAction={() => saveChanges()}>Save changes</Button>;
}
```

## Locale

`BreezeProvider` takes a BCP 47 language tag, such as `en-GB` or `fr-FR`. Components use it for accessible interactions and text direction.

## Appearance

Components follow the operating system colour scheme by default. Add `AppearanceControl` anywhere below the provider to let people choose Light, Auto or Dark:

```tsx
import {
  AppearanceControl,
  type Appearance,
  BreezeProvider,
} from '@motech-development/breeze-ui';
import { useState } from 'react';

export function Root() {
  const [appearance, setAppearance] = useState<Appearance>('automatic');

  return (
    <BreezeProvider
      appearance={appearance}
      locale="en-GB"
      onAppearanceChange={setAppearance}
    >
      <AppearanceControl />
      <App />
    </BreezeProvider>
  );
}
```

Breeze UI does not save the choice in browser storage. To remember it between visits, store the value passed to `onAppearanceChange` and pass it back as `appearance` (or `defaultAppearance`).

## Customisation

Change a component's appearance through the props documented on its page. `className`, `style` and theme overrides are not supported.

